## ABSTRACT
Rechargeable non-aqueous $L i{\cdot}O_{2}$ battery is regarded as one of the most promising energy-storage technologies on account of its high energy density. It is believed that the rational design of three-dimensional (3D) architecture for catalyst is a key factor for the remarkable performance. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) derived materials possess excellent architecture, which is beneficial for $L i{\cdot}O_{2}$ batteries. In this work, ZIF-67 is used as precursor template and calcinated under different temperature to produce $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ crystals. When the anneal treatment is under $350^{\circ}\mathsf{C},$ , the derived $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanocage holds the most complete skeleton, which provides better charge transfer ability as well as ${{\mathsf O}_{2}}$ and ${\mathsf{L i}}^{+}$ diffusion. Meanwhile, the $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanocage owns more oxygen vacancies, offering more active sites. With the synergistic effect of nanocage structure and active sites, the $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanocage stably delivers a large specific capacity of $15,500\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}}^{-1}$ as well as a long cycle-life of 132 cycles at limited discharge capacity of $1,000\ m{\mathsf{A h{\cdot g}}}^{-1}$ under discharge/charge current density of $0.5\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ .
## KEYWORDS
$L i{\cdot}O_{2}$ batteries, metal-organic framework (MOF)-derived, $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanocage, $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ polyhedron, $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ particle
## Introduction
Rechargeable non-aqueous Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ battery is regarded as one of the most promising energy-storage technologies which may meet the demand of driving mileages of electric vehicles because of its theoretical high energy density of $3,860\mathrm{Wh}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ [1]. However, there are still many barriers impeding the utilization of Li- $O_{2}$ batteries in reality, such as poor roundtrip ability, low energy efficiency and unpromising cathode performance as well as stability [2–4]. The working process of Li- $O_{2}$ batteries depends on the reaction between $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and oxygen [5–7]. It is believed that the well-designed catalystcathode could provide enough path for the diffusion of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and guarantee plenty space for the growth of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ during discharging, resulting in a long cycle-life of Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries [8–10]. Thus, the architecture of cathode catalysts is one of the most important factors to promote cycling duration of Li- $O_{2}$ batteries.
As reported in the literature, many types of materials have been applied as catalysts for $\mathrm{Li}{\bf-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries. Noble metal catalysts as well as their oxides (such as Pt [11], Pd [12], Au [13], $\mathrm{Ag}$ [14], and $\mathrm{Ru/RuO}_{2}$ [15–17]) are chosen as cathode catalyst for their excellent oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) performance, which could play a positive role in enhancing cycling life as well as Coulombic efficiency of $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries. Nevertheless, the high price of precious metals prevents further applications. In this context, cost-effective transition metal based oxides (such as $\mathrm{{MnO}}_{2}$ [18, 19], $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [20, 21], and NiO [22]) phosphides [23], layered double hydroxides (LDHs) [24] and other compounds [25, 26] are proposed and widely studied. Among these catalysts, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is chosen for its relatively high redox activity and favorable catalytic activity for both ORR and OER [27]. Hydrothermal method is the most commonly used method to prepare Co based catalysts. However, the morphologies of catalysts synthesized by hydrothermal method are mostly nanowires or nanosheets, i.e. one-dimensional (1D) or two-dimensional (2D) materials [18, 28]. Considering the working mechanism of Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries, we decided to study a catalyst with three-dimensional (3D) structure, and expect that the 3D structure could provide more space and active sites for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ deposition and decomposition.
In the last two decades, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have become a rapidly expanding research area because the frameworks of MOFs are designable via a modular self-assembly process, hereby the 3D structure of this kind of materials as well as particle size or pore size could be easily adjusted [29–31]. Very recently, some MOFderived materials walk into the horizon of Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries because of their unique structure and excellent catalytic performance [32, 33]. Kim and co-workers directly used prepared MnCo-MOF-74 as cathode catalyst without any further annealing [34]. This material possessed excellent porous structure as well as the complementation from both Co- and Mn-metal clusters. However, the electrochemical performance of untreated MOF was barely satisfactory. In order to settle the poor electric conductivity of MOFs, more conductive carbon was added into slurry. During the roundtrip test, MnCo-MOF-74 catalyst only acquired 44 cycles with a limited discharge capacity of $1{,}000\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ at current density of $200\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . In order to enhance electrochemical performance of MOF, further optimization treatment such as thermal treatment should be undertaken. Tan and co-workers synthesized MOF-derived biphasic N-doped cobalt $@$ graphene heterostructure material as catalyst for Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries [35]. Nevertheless, after annealing at excessive high temperature, the skeleton of MOF breaks with multiple-capsule heterostructures left. The electric conductivity as well as catalytic activity were advanced, while the electrochemical performance of $_{\mathrm{Li-O}_{2}}$ batteries still required to be improved. When the capacity was limited at $1.0\ m A\mathrm{h}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , the battery could only obtain a stable cycling of 30 cycles.
MOF and MOF-derived materials are promising although a huge improvement is required. Among all kinds of MOFs, ZIF-67 was chosen mainly because it is a Co-based materials which is easy to synthesis. Like other MOFs, the metal ions distributed evenly in the robust ZIF-67 structures, making ZIF-67 a promising precursor for the synthesis of metallic oxides [36]. By controlling the heat treatment condition, the dodecahedron skeleton and porosity of ZIF-67 could be maintained in the finial products. The unique 3D structure of ZIF-67 derived catalysts could provide excellent structure for the reaction involved in Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ battery as well as $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ diffusion.
In this work, ZIF-67 was chosen as a precursor to synthesize $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocage under different calcination temperature. When the heat treatment was under $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocage was obtained with most complete skeleton and porosity. On the account of the synergistic effect of unique structure and excellent catalytic performance, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocage stably delivered a large specific capacity of $13{,}500\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ as well as a long cycle-life of 132 cycles at a limited discharge capacity of $1{,}000\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ under charge/discharge current density of $0.5\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ .
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Electrode preparation
All chemicals were directly used after purchase without any further treatment.
Synthesis of ZIF-67. In a typical synthesis progress, 6 mmol $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ was weighted and dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol and $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol (solution A), at the same time 12 mmol 2-methylimidazole (2-MIM) was weighted and dissolved in another $20~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol and $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol (solution B). Both solutions were ultrasonically treated for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . After that, solution A was rapidly poured into solution B when solution B was under intensely stirring. Then, the mixed solution was stirred for $10~\mathsf{s}.$ , followed with sonication for another $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . The final solution together with container was transformed to an oven and kept at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for more than $20\mathrm{~h~}$ . The violet precursor was collected and washed with ethanol by centrifugation for more than 3 times and dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. In the end, ZIF-67 precursor was obtained.
Synthesis of ZIF-67 derived $C o_{3}O_{4}$ based catalysts. Obtained ZIF-67 precursor was heat treated at 350, 500 and $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ in air to obtain corresponding catalysts, named Co-cage, Co-polyhedron and Co-particle, respectively (according to the morphologies of three catalysts). The rising rate was $1{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/s$ .
## 2.2 Electrode characterization
The structures and morphologies of the Co-cage, Co-polyhedron and Co-particle were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Quanta 200F) as well as transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM 2100 LaB6). The crystal structures of three catalysts were examined by a Bruker D8 X-ray diffraction using $\mathrm{Cu-K}\alpha$ radiation. And the chemical valence state information of Cocage was operated using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS,
ESCALAB MARK II spherical analyzer) with a $\mathbf{Mg}$ anode $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray source. Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) analysis was used to measure specific surface area of three catalysts with an ASAP 2460 Analyzer. Thermogravimetric (TG) analysis was carried out with a Mettler-Toledo TGA/DSC $^{3+}$ thermal analyzer under air flow between 100 and $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ For the characterization of ex-SEM, cathodes after 10th discharge and after 10th charge process were unloaded and washed with dimethyl carbonate (DMC) to remove abundant glassfiber. For the characterization of discharge product with TEM, cathode after 10th discharge was unloaded, washed, then catalysts were exfoliated from carbon cloth through ultrasound.
## 2.3  Electrode preparation and Li- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf O}_{2}$ cell assembly
Catalyst powders $(80~\mathrm{~wt.\%})$ , acetylene black $(10~\mathrm{~wt.\%})$ and polyvinylidene fluoride binder (PVDF, $10\ \mathrm{wt.\%},$ were mixed in a solution of N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) to obtain a slurry. The cathodes of $_{\mathrm{Li-O}_{2}}$ batteries were made by smearing the slurry uniformly on the surface of tailored carbon cloth $(0.8\mathrm{cm}\times0.8\mathrm{cm})$ with a loading mass of $0.6{-}0.8~\mathrm{mg}$ , followed by drying in an oven overnight to remove the NMP at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . Metallic Li foil was used as anode, and glassfiber as separator. The electrolyte was $1\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ lithiumbis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (LiTFSI) dissolved in tetraethylene glycol dimethyl ether (TEGDME). Before assembling, every oxygen cathode should be dried over $6\mathrm{{h}}$ to shed the influence of water. A glove box filled with ultra-highly pure argon was needed for the assembling of these non-aqueous Li- $O_{2}$ batteries, guaranteeing the concentration of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ was below $0.5\mathrm{ppm}$ . After packaging, the cells were sealed in a homemade glass chamber filled with highpurity oxygen.
## 2.4 Electrochemical measurements
Electrochemical performances of non-aqueous $\mathrm{Li}{\bf-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries were carried out on top-holing R2032-type coin cells. Galvanostatic discharging and charging tests were operated on a battery test system (LAND-V34, Land Electronic Co. Ltd., Wuhan, China) at room temperature after $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ rest. The values of current density and specific capacity of the oxygen cathodes relied on the total weight of catalysts covered on the surface of carbon cloth. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) was tested on an electrochemical working station (AMETEK Solartron 1260/1287) at scan rate of $0.1~\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}s^{-1}$ in the range between 2.0 and $4.3~\mathrm{V}.$ The measurements of electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were performed at the original state, 1st-discharged state and 1st-charged state at a limited specific capacity of $1{,}000\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ in a frequency range of $10^{-3}$ to $10^{4}\mathrm{Hz}$ .
For the OER experience of different catalysts, a glass-carbon electrode (disk area $0.071\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) was used as substrate of working electrode, counter electrode was Pt flake and reference electrode was SCE. Before testing, glass-carbon electrode was polished with $0.3~\mathrm{mm}$ alumina slurry to afford a mirror finish. For the preparation of catalyst ink, $3\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst with $950~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of solvent ( $50\%$ deionized (DI) water $50\%$ ethonal, v:v) and $50\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $5\mathrm{wt.\%}$ Nafion was mixed ultrasonically for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Then, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ slurry of catalyst was dripped on the surface of glassy-carbon disk. The OER test was operated in $0.1\mathrm{{M}}$ NaOH aqueous solution.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1  Characterization of MOF-derived catalysts
The XRD pattern of as-prepared and simulated ZIF-67 was shown in Fig. 2(a). The characteristic peaks of as-prepared ZIF-67 perfectly match the simulated pattern, which means ZIF-67 was successfully synthesized. According to the TG result and differential thermal analysis (DTA) curve (Fig. 2(c)), the most important weight loss happened between 325 and $380~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{:}}$ , especially at about $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ In this consideration, 350, 500 and $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were chosen as calcination temperature to treat the ZIF-67. Moreover, the weight loss after $380^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ remained at $36\%$ of its original weight (Fig. 2(c)), which also demonstrated that the obtained products are $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ (based on the calculation of molecular weight of ZIF-67 and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ ) [30, 38]. XRD results revealed all of the products were $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ crystal (Fig. 2(b)), which perfectly match the pattern of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (PDF 42-1467). Meanwhile, an obvious trend existed that with the rising of pyrolysis temperature, the corresponding peaks became shaper and narrower, which demonstrated that with the increase of temperature, the crystallinity of particles became better and the size of particles became smaller.
The porosity of Co-cage, Co-polyhedron and Co-particle were quantified by measuring the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherm (Figs. 2(d)–2(f)). Co-cage and Co-polyhedron presented typical type IV isotherm with a hysteresis loop in the range of ca. 0.75–1.0 $P/P_{0},$ indicating the existence of mesopores in the sample [39]. The main pore size of Co-cage appeared at ca. $26~\mathrm{nm}$ (in the range of mesopores) (Fig. S2 in the ESM), while the main pore sizes of Co-polyhedron appeared at $20\mathrm{nm}$ . However, Co-particle presented typical type II isotherm, meaning the absence of pores. Moreover, the BET surface area of these three catalysts was also compared. Co-cage owned largest surface area of $36\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , followed with $31\mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ for Co-polyhedron and only $6\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for Co-particle. The reactions of Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries during discharge and change happen on the surface of catalysts, hence larger surface area may significantly contain more active sites for discharge/charge reaction, thereby providing higher activity during cycling tests. According to the above results, $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was an optimal calcination temperature because this temperature yielded well-remained structure and the largest BET surface area.
The corresponding chemical states of the Co-cage, Co-polyhedron and Co-particle were further investigated by XPS measurement. The fine-scanned Co 2p spectra are fitted to investigate the electronic states of Co atoms with different valences (Figs. 3(a)–3(c)).
Two major peaks appeared respectively at 780.0 and $795.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ with a spin-orbit splitting of $15.1\ \mathrm{eV},$ which belonged to the $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ orbitals of Co ions in $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ [40, 41]. Both the major peaks could be further deconvoluted into two subsets. One subset situates at 779.70 and $794.80\ \mathrm{eV}$ for ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ , and the other one locates at 781.35 and $796.45\mathrm{~eV}$ for ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ . The $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ ratio in the Co-cage is calculated to be 0.83, which is higher than that of Co-polyhedron (0.79) and Co-particle (0.75), indicating part of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ ions on Co-cage surface has been reduced to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions. As a result, more $\mathbf{Co^{2+}}$ ions exposed on the surface of Co-cage along with the formation of oxygen vacancies. Moreover, the XPS analysis of O 1s further confirmed the existence of oxygen vacancies (Figs. 3(d)–3(f)). The fine-scanned O 1s spectra could be deconvoluted into four distinct peaks. In detail, the peaks at 530.2, 531.4, 532.4 and $533.7\mathrm{eV}$ could be ascribed to metal oxide (O1), oxygen vacancies on the surface (O2), surface-adsorbed oxygen (O3) and surface-adsorbed molecular water (O4), respectively [42]. The O2 ratio of Co-cage (0.27) is also higher than that of Co-polyhedron (0.18) and Co-particle (0.13), indicating that the Co-cage possessed more oxygen vacancies than the other catalysts. XPS results demonstrate that more ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ is reduced to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ under $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ annealing process, thereby creating more oxygen vacancies on the surface of Co-cage. It has reported that oxygen vacancies have a positive effect on the reversible deposition and decomposition of discharge products $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , and could effectively promote ORR/OER of $\mathrm{Li}{\bf-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries and enhance cycling life. Therefore, the oxygen vacancies appeared on the Co-cage sample are expected to promote the catalytic activities towards both ORR and OER.
## 3.2  Electrochemical properties and performance of the Li-O2 batteries
To understand the OER behavior of the three catalysts, linear potential scanning studies were carried out in 0.1 M NaOH aqueous solution (Fig. S3 in th ESM). The potential at $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for OER on Co-cage is $1.69\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE which is lower than that of Co-polyhedron (1.77 V vs. RHE) and Co-particle (1.81 V vs. RHE). Meanwhile, the Tafel slopes of Co-cage, Co-polyhedron and Coparticle are also compared. The Tafel slope of Co-cage is calculated to be $91.0\mathrm{mV.dec}^{-1}$ , which is much lower than that of Copolyhedron $(105.2\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ and Co-particle $(110.5~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ . The comparison of both onset potential and Tafel slope confirmed that the OER performance of Co-cage is better than that of Copolyhedron and $\mathrm{Co}$ -particle.
Electrochemical performance of Co-cage, Co-polyhedron and Co-particle catalysts was further tested in assembled Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ cells. Figure 4(a) was a digital photo of a button type Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ battery demo which could light up total 21 red light emitting diodes $(1.8{\mathrm{~V}})$ , spelling the abbreviation for China University of Petroleum-Beijing (CUP). The CV curves of Co-cage, Co-polyhedron and Co-particle were compared in Fig. 4(e). Both Co-cage and Co-polyhedron electrodes displayed a strong reduction peak at around $2.24\mathrm{V}$ during the cathodic scan, indicating the excellent catalytic performance for ORR. It was worth notice that the reduction peak current density of the Co-cage was much higher than that of Co-polyhedron electrode, at the same time the reduction peak of Co-particle is too weak to be observed. This trend indicated that Co-cage obtains best ORR performance. During the succeeding anodic scan process, a relative strong oxidation peak could be observed at $3.45\mathrm{~V~}$ for Co-cage. In contrast, Co-polyhedron and Co-particle electrodes only show weak OER peaks. This appearance indicated that the Co-cage electrode contains a better OER performance. These CV features demonstrated that the Co-flower electrode exhibits superior OER and ORR properties.
To gain an insight into these $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ based $\mathrm{Li}{\bf-O}_{2}$ batteries, the Co-cage, Co-polyhedron and Co-particle electrodes were conducted under deep discharge/charge conditions with various current densities of 100, 200, 500 and $1{,}000\ \mathrm{mA{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ , as Figs. $4(\mathrm{b})\AA{-}4(\mathrm{d})$ show. The cut-off potentials for discharge process and charge process were 2.2 and $4.3~\mathrm{V},$ respectively. Samples treated at excessively high temperature lead to structure broken and lowest BET area, resulting in the lower specific capacity and larger overpotential. Typically, when conducted at mild current density of $100\ \mathrm{\mA{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , Co-cage obtains the highest specific capacity of $15{,}500\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ and highest charge capacity of $14{,}200\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ , resulting in a Coulombic efficiency of $91\%$ . As for Co-polyhedron, the discharge capacity and charge capacity decrease to 13,200 and $10,600\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}};$ , and the Coulombic efficiency drops to $80\%$ . For Co-particle, the discharge capacity and charge capacity are even smaller, presenting only 10,600 and $6{,}800\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ , with the worst Coulombic efficiency of $64\%$ . When conducted at low current density of $100\ \mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , the discharge plateaus of three catalysts are almost the same, sustaining at around $2.76~\mathrm{V}.$ However, the charge plateau of Co-cage $(4.0\mathrm{~V})$ is lower than that of Co-polyhedron (4.1 V) and Co-particle (4.15 V), thus Co-cage has lower overpotential. When operated at higher current density of $200\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , Co-cage could still sustain a discharge capacity of $12,400\ \mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ , much higher than that of Co-polyhedron $(10,700\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}) $ ) and Co-particle $(8,440\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}},$ ). The discharge plateau of Co-cage remains stable at around $2.76~\mathrm{V},$ while the discharge plateaus of Co-polyhedron and Co-particle drop to 2.69 and $2.60\mathrm{V},$ respectively. When the current density rise to as high as 500 and $1{,}000\ \mathrm{mA{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ , Co-cage could still obtain large discharge capacity of 5,400 and $2{,}300\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ , respectively. However, Co-polyhedron could only sustain discharge capacity of 3,200 and $1{,}200\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ . When it comes to Co-particle, only 2,300 and $400\mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ could be sustained. Rate performance demonstrated the priority of excellent catalytic performance Co-cage catalyst.
The roundtrip performance of these three batteries was tested with a fixed capacity of $1{,}000\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ under the current density of $500\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ as Figs. 4(f)–4(h) show. The discharge plateau and charge plateau of three catalysts for the 1st cycle were almost the same, sustaining at about 2.75 and $4.0\mathrm{V}.$ For Co-cage, the discharge plateau maintains stable for the first 100 cycles without obvious decrease (Fig. 4(f)). In the end, Co-cage holds a long lifetime of 132 times before discharge plateau drops to $2.2\mathrm{V}$ (Fig. 4(i)). However, the discharge plateau of Co-polyhedron and Co-particle could not keep stable for such long time, resulting in a roundtrip performance of 85 times and 50 times, respectively. When compared with the other MOF based or MOF-derived materials-based catalysts, Co-cage owns best capacity and best cycle numbers (Fig. 4(j)), proving Co-cage was an efficient cathode catalyst for Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries. A comparison of cycle numbers between this work and reported $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ based Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries is shown in Table S1 in the ESM. Compared with other $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ based catalysts, our work also shows outstanding performance.
## 3.3  Analysis of oxygen electrodes and impedance variation after discharged and charged
To investigate the process of formation and decomposition of discharge products, ex-situ SEM and TEM analysis were carried out on air cathodes after 10th discharge and after $10\mathrm{{th}}$ recharge. As Figs. 5(a) and 5(b) show, there is evident in changes of the morphologies for Co-cage-based air cathodes. After 10th discharge, the surface of Co-cage nanocages was coated with $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and the edges of dodecahedrons became thicker (measuring $0.13\upmu\mathrm{m}\dot{}$ ). The rod shape materials in Fig. 5(a) are glass fibers from separator. Whereas, almost all $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ on the surface had disappeared after recharge. At the same time, the edges of dodecahedrons became thin and clear again (measuring $0.06\upmu\mathrm{m}\dot{},$ ). Moreover, TEM images and corresponding SEAD patterns confirmed the existence $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ crystal. When the selected area was in the middle of Co-cage, the SEAD pattern was similar with that of Co-cage catalysts, which was corresponding to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . An enlarged SEAD image was shown in Fig. S4 in the ESM. Despite the rings of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}.$ , there appeared a new ring, which could be indexed to the (102) plane of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . This result demonstrates that not only the edges but also the middle areas are covered with $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The diffraction rings of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ were clearer mainly because $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ only formed on the surface of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ and the amount of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ was larger than that of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Moreover, when the selected area was on the margin of Co-cage, the SEAD pattern was corresponding to $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , which gave evidence of the presence of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The SEM images of Co-polyhedron and Co-particle after discharging process and after charging process were shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM. For both of them, the morphologies after charging and after discharging could not be distinguished, revealing the irreversible discharging products covered on the surface of Co-polyhedron and Co-particle. These characterizations demonstrated the electrocatalytic performance of Co-cage was better than Co-polyhedron and Co-particle.
EIS analysis was carried out to record the variation of impedance of the Co-cage, Co-polyhedron and Co-particle based Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries at different discharge/charge stages (Figs. 5(d)–5(f)). The three Nyquist plots were fitted by using the equivalent circuit in Fig. 5(e) and the fitting results of the semicircle were summarized in Table 1.
In the equivalent circuit, $R_{\mathrm{o}}$ indicates the ohm resistance of $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries. And the semicircle in the middle-frequency region is represented by $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ (the charge transfer resistance) and $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ (double layer capacitance). And the line in the low frequency region is represented as $W$ (Warburg impedance), which is due to resistance against oxygen diffusion to electrode surface and Li ion diffusion [43]. As the fitting results show, in the different stages of cells, the change in $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ was much more obvious than $R_{\mathrm{o}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{dl}}.$ . Typically, the impedance of three batteries increases after discharge on account of the formation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and decreases after recharge due to the decomposition of discharge products. Specifically, as for Co-cage, the impedance of $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ increases from 67 to $151\ \Omega$ after discharge process and returns to $89~\Omega$ after charge process. The impedance almost recovers to initial state, suggesting the discharge products are almost totally decomposed. As for Co-polyhedron and Co-particle, the impedance of $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ increases much more than Co-cage, from 79 to $207\Omega$ for Co-polyhedron and from 81 to $312\Omega$ for Co-particle. After the charging process, the impedance still remains 141 and 154 $\Omega$ , respectively. The EIS results of these three catalysts reveals that after the charging process, the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ on the surface of Co-cage have been mostly decomposed. As comparison, the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ on the surface of Co-polyhedron and Co-particle were partially decomposed after the charging process, resulting in the high impedance of $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ . Considering the realistic meaning of $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ (the charge transfer resistance), this trend demonstrates that the Co-cage could provide better charge transfer as it maintains complete skeleton. As for the other two catalysts, particles with more interfaces may increase the charge transfer resistance. The EIS results were consistent with the SEM images of electrodes after the discharge/charge process, which also demonstrated the advantage of Co-cage with high electrocatalytic as cathode for Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries [44].
## 4 Conclusion
In this work, ZIF-67 was successful synthesized and annealed under different temperature to obtain catalysts with different morphologies. XRD results demonstrate that Co-cage, Co-polyhedron and Coparticle are $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ crystal and the SEM images prove that Co-cage sustains complete skeleton of ZIF-67, nevertheless the structure of Co-polyhedron is collapsed into particles and the morphology of Co-particle is tiny particles. Meanwhile, through comparing the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ and $\sigma_{\omega}$ of $_{\mathrm{Li-O}_{2}}$ batteries with different catalysts, the unique structure of Co-cage has been proved to guarantee a better charge transfer ability as well as $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ diffusion. The XPS analysis of three catalysts demonstrates that that Co-cage has more oxygen vacancies on its surface, which could provide more active sites and boost the electrochemical property. The linear sweep voltammogram (LSV) tests also give evidence that the Co-cage has better OER performance. The outstanding performance of Co-cage is attributed to the synergistic effect of unique nanocage structure and active sites. Hence, Co-cage exhibits largest specific capacity of $15{,}500\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ under current density of $\mathrm{\bar{100}\ m A{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ at cut-off potential of $2.2~\mathrm{V}.$ As for Copolyhedron and Co-particle, the discharge capacity is only 132,100 and $10,600\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ . Moreover, when conducted at a fixed specific capacity of $1,000\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ under current density of $500\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ Co-cage could sustain a cycling performance as long as 132 times. Under the same condition, Co-polyhedron and Co-particle could only hold a roundtrip performance of 85 times and 50 times, respectively. Through the controlling of the heat treatment conditions, the skeleton of ZIF-67 could be remained in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocage. Our research demonstrates that ZIF-67 derived cathode catalyst with complete architecture is benefit for Li- $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ battery.
